{
  "about": "The facts the talk library may test and say (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, section 1). Doc 84, Phase 10 (§11's slots): surname; called (what the one speaking calls this one: their first name between kin, friends and those who know it, a Master or Mistress and the surname across a class gap in Ridgemere, else nothing, and #other.called# says the speaker's #address#); spouse_word (husband or wife); late (the kin last mourned, dead); friend and rival (the closest, affinity 40 or more, and the worst, -25 or less, living); boss and hand (over and under at work); workplace (the work's place by name), trade_words (the post as its title says it), street (the street home opens onto), from_town (where they grew up, by name), away (grew up elsewhere than they live), dialect (the grammar they speak); pair.echo and pair.echo_of (§12.4: one of the two wears a mantle last worn by kin of theirs whom the other knew well, and that wolf's name). Doc 84, Phase 6: a person's mood and mood_why (§8.8: the last days' events), likes and dislikes (\"list\": any number of opinions.json's items, self.likes=honey_cakes holding when the list has it) and the words of each kind liked (pastime_words, food_words, weather_words, thing_words; dislike_words), stance, stance_why and stance_strength (their side on the issue a talk is on: conv.issue), speaks (the accent a speaker uses toward the one spoken to: accommodation, §10.6, read before accent); each issue's op.<issue>, op_why.<issue> and op_strength.<issue>, and the pair's pair.agree.<issue>, come from opinions.json. pair.same_speech: the two grew up speaking the same dialect. world.arrest (within a day) and world.mood (the town's: frames.json's moods). topic.subject_sex and topic.victim_sex give #topic.they# and #topic.victim_they# (their, them...). A list is the values a fact may take; \"text\" is words to say, testable with any value; \"number\" may be compared (self.age>=60). A person's facts are read as self.X and other.X (the speaker and the one spoken to) or a.X and b.X (in a scene, the opener and the answerer); the rest by their namespace (here.place), or alone where only one namespace has the name (place). A misspelt fact or value in a rule is refused when the library loads. Doc 84, Phase 12 (§13.1): here.came_in (the player came in within two minutes, and the keeper looked up: the window greets a customer), here.newcomer (a newcomer whose welcome at this inn is due: the greeting is the welcome), topic.asked (on talk.player: what a resident overheard the player asking after in open chat, a quarter of a day, in words: \"asking the way to the Grey Gull\"). Where each comes from in the world, and how its values are worked out: Docs/Design/62-talk.md, appendix.",
